= Thought suppression =

== Overview ==
'''Thought suppression''' is the deliberate attempt to prevent a target thought from entering or remaining in conscious awareness.

It is studied as a form of mental control.

The scientific literature does not support the simple universal rule that trying not to think about something must always make the thought stronger.

== White-bear paradigm ==
Classic experiments asked participants not to think about a white bear and to report when the thought occurred.

Some studies found a rebound after the suppression period: the target thought appeared more often after suppression than in comparison conditions.

These results became a major basis for [[Ironic process theory]].

== Immediate suppression ==
During an active suppression period, people can often reduce reported target thoughts.

Effects vary with task, strategy, target content, cognitive load, measurement, and individual differences.

Therefore immediate effectiveness and later rebound need to be analyzed separately.

== Rebound and hyperaccessibility ==
Older literature reported rebound or increased accessibility under some conditions, especially when mental control is strained.

Contemporary reviews have questioned how general and robust these paradoxical effects are across paradigms.

The evidence supports conditional claims rather than an automatic backfire rule.

== Thought suppression and memory suppression ==
Thought suppression concerns keeping content out of current awareness.

Memory-suppression paradigms such as think/no-think attempt to interrupt retrieval of learned memories.

The literatures overlap in mental control but use different tasks and outcomes.

== Adaptiveness ==
Suppression can be helpful, neutral, or harmful depending on context, strategy, duration, target, and individual.

Correlations between habitual suppression and distress do not by themselves show that suppression caused the distress.

== Practical interpretation ==
The popular instruction “don't think of a pink elephant” illustrates difficulty in controlling thought, but “pink elephant bias” is not a standard scientific construct.

== Related concepts ==
[[Attention]], [[Working memory]], [[Cognitive load]], and [[Ironic process theory]] are central neighboring concepts in the study of mental control.

